book.chapter(36, "DSLtk.hpp: sources, parsers, and diagnostics")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A parser cursor borrows source"
.pp
The native combinator core uses ParsecInput, ExpectedResult, and Parser.
ParsecInput holds a string_view source and a byte position. Peek inspects
the next byte, consume advances, eof distinguishes actual exhaustion, and
get_span returns the source interval consumed since a saved position.
Because string_view does not own storage, the source must remain alive
through parsing and through any retained views derived from it.
.pp
Peek uses a zero-byte sentinel at end of input, so call eof when a real
embedded zero must be distinguished from exhaustion. This is a general
systems-programming lesson: a convenient sentinel is not an adequate
binary-input contract by itself. The separate BinaryStream facility later
in the book provides an explicitly byte-oriented interface for binary work.
Choose deliberately rather than treating every parser cursor as interchangeable.
.sh 2 "Parsers return values and structured failures"
.lua
book.code("cpp", [=[#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <cassert>
int main() {
    auto parser = dsl::ch('a') & dsl::ch('b');
    auto result = dsl::run_parser(parser, "ab");
    assert(result.value.has_value());
    auto bad = dsl::run_parser(parser, "ax");
    assert(!bad.value.has_value());
}]=], "A complete native parser composition")
book.code("d-fragment", [=[auto parser = ch('a') & ch('b');
auto result = run_parser(parser, "ab");]=], "The corresponding D combinator shape")
.endlua
.pp
A custom native parser can be built with dsl::parser around a callable taking
ParsecInput by reference and returning ExpectedResult of its value type.
Success carries a value; failure carries a position, failure kind, and
expected labels. This makes failure composition explicit rather than
requiring every ordinary mismatch to throw a native exception. Keep source
positions in one consistent byte coordinate system throughout the parse.
.sh 2 "Commitment is part of the native grammar contract"
.pp
ParseFailureKind distinguishes soft and committed failures. An alternative
can restore the cursor and try another branch after a soft failure. A
committed failure prevents that fallback. Sequence promotes a later soft
failure when earlier work consumed input, preserving the fact that the
grammar has already entered a construct. Optional and repetition must also
respect commitment rather than swallowing a malformed partially consumed item.
.pp
This behavior differs from simply assuming every failed parser backtracks
unconditionally. It also differs from the convenient MetaML Parsec wrapper
described earlier. When porting a grammar between interfaces, test shared
prefixes and partially recognized constructs. A missing closing delimiter
after a valid opening delimiter often deserves a committed error rather
than a fallback that pretends the entire construct was absent.
.lua
book.algorithm("Alternative with commitment", {
    "Save the initial cursor.",
    "Try the first parser.",
    "On success, return its value.",
    "On committed failure, propagate its error.",
    "On soft failure, restore the cursor and try the next alternative.",
    "Merge competing failure expectations at the furthest position."
})
.endlua
.sh 2 "Diagnostics should retain the best evidence"
.pp
ParseError records the furthest useful position and expected labels.
When failures occur at different positions, the farther one is generally
more informative. At the same position, merge labels while preserving
deterministic order and removing duplicates. A language-level diagnostic can
then connect the parser's expectation to a named source span and an
actionable message. Avoid reporting only whichever speculative branch happened
to fail last.
.pp
The generic Diagnostic and Span facilities are reusable across languages.
MetaML assigns its own M-prefixed codes and language-specific messages while
using shared source-location infrastructure. A toolkit must not embed
MetaML-specific grammar assumptions merely because MetaML is one consumer.
Conversely, a language should not duplicate a generic location or diagnostic
mechanism when the reusable layer can express the needed contract cleanly.
.sh 2 "Distinguish parser families"
.pp
DSLtk includes ordinary combinators, PEG-oriented facilities, static byte
regexes, and an explicit CFG parser. These solve different problems.
Ordered-choice grammars, longest-match tokenization, and context-free
derivation do not share identical ambiguity or recursion rules. Identify
which subsystem an example uses before applying advice about left recursion,
commitment, or full-input matching. A common header does not imply a single
universal parser execution model.
.pp
For a language frontend, establish separate contracts for lexing, parsing,
and semantic validation. Tokenization can recognize a digit sequence without
proving numeric range. Parsing can establish a declaration's shape without
resolving its references. Semantic validation can reject an otherwise valid
tree. Retaining spans across those stages makes the eventual error useful
without forcing the lexer to perform every later analysis prematurely.
.sh 2 "Resource and recovery tests"
.pp
Test malformed prefixes, repeated optional constructs, and deeply nested
input. A repeated parser must either consume input or terminate according
to its contract; repeated empty success cannot define useful progress.
Backtracking can multiply work, and recursion can consume stack even when
the source is short. Measure actual parser behavior rather than inferring
linear complexity merely from a compositional API.
.pp
Also test cursor restoration. A soft failure that leaves a cursor advanced
can corrupt the next alternative, producing a misleading later error.
A parser returning a view into a temporary string can appear correct until
the next allocation overwrites the storage. These native lifetime and
state invariants deserve targeted tests because a successful parse on one
literal does not exercise them.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read ParsecInput, ParseError, merge_error, Parser, and run_parser in DSLtk.hpp,
plus examples/05-parser-basics.cpp and the parser composition examples.
Compare the D definitions in DSLtk.d. Build two alternatives with a shared
prefix and observe where commitment should occur. Then write a custom
identifier parser that retains a span, documenting who owns the source
storage and how long the returned view remains valid.
